abstract: >-
  Most recent work on fake news detection reaches for transformer models, which need a GPU and long training
  runs. We ask how much accuracy is lost when those resources are unavailable. We combine an XGBoost model over TFIDF
  and part-of-speech features with a TextCNN over frozen GloVe embeddings, and merge the two by soft voting. On the
  WELFake dataset of 72,134 articles the ensemble reaches 97.81%± 0.20 accuracy across five random seeds, using 256,432
  learned parameters (5.26M including the frozen embeddings it loads but never updates) against BERT-base’s 110 million,
  and trains in 14.9 minutes on an Intel Core i7-1165G7 with no GPU. A published BERT result on this data is 1.1 points
  higher, though from a different split, so that comparison is indicative only. Two controls then qualify our number
  sharply. Deleting wire-service and publisher tokens costs just 0.82 points, but withholding an entire publisher from
  training raises the ensemble’s false positive rate from 3.3% to 21.7%, and legitimate articles carrying a wire
  dateline are misclassified 51 times less often than those without one. Benchmark accuracy on this corpus is therefore
  substantially a function of source provenance. Part-of-speech features add nothing the lexical representation does not
  already supply.
